// Where donated money goes.
//
// JustUs never takes custody: a donation is a Stripe Connect *destination
// charge*, so the full amount transfers to the recipient's connected account and
// only the platform fee comes back to us. That part of the promise (terms §4) is
// true regardless of who the recipient is. This file holds the account money
// lands in; the donation ledger itself is `Donation` in case.prisma, next to the
// case it funds.
//
// **The recipient is the representing law firm's operating account.** Donations
// land in the business checking account of the firm that represents the case, and
// the attorney moves them into their own IOLTA/trust account as an advance of
// fees under their state bar's rules. Two things follow, and both are why this is
// the model rather than paying the plaintiff:
//
//   - The platform never routes crowd-raised legal fees to a private individual,
//     which is what created the liability the previous model carried.
//   - The trust-accounting obligation lands where the professional duty already
//     is — with the attorney, under bar supervision — instead of on JustUs.
//
// A trust account is deliberately *not* what Stripe is pointed at: IOLTA accounts
// carry restrictions banks reject an automated debit relationship against, so the
// operating account is both the compliant and the technically viable destination.
// To Stripe this is an ordinary business receiving funds — an EIN, a firm name,
// and a business checking account through the standard hosted flow.

/// A Stripe Connect (Express) account donations can be sent to — the law firm's
/// operating account, held by the attorney who onboarded it.
///
/// **One account per case, not per person and not per firm.** A firm running three
/// cases onboards three times and holds three `acct_…` ids. That is more work for the
/// attorney than a single firm-wide account, and it is deliberate:
///
///   - **Funds never mix.** Each case's donations land in their own balance, so a
///     chargeback or refund on one case cannot be taken out of another case's money.
///     Sharing a balance means one donor's dispute reduces a different client's fund.
///   - **Separate bank routing.** Each account carries its own external bank account,
///     so a firm can route one case's funds to one internal account and another's
///     elsewhere. A single connected account has one destination and no way to split.
///   - **Clean per-case accounting** on Stripe's side as well as ours, with no
///     reconciling of one balance against our ledger to work out whose money is whose.
///
/// The cost is a full Stripe onboarding — identity, EIN, bank account, review — per
/// case, and an Express dashboard per case rather than one. That is accepted as the
/// price of the isolation above.
///
/// Held by a `User` because the account is opened by a verifiable individual: an
/// attorney with a bar number on file, who bears the professional obligation for the
/// money afterwards.
///
/// Every column except `stripeAccountId` is a **cache of Stripe's own view**,
/// written only by the `account.updated` webhook and the initial create call.
/// Nothing here is form-editable, because anyone who could set `chargesEnabled`
/// themselves could open donations against an account that cannot receive them.
model PayoutAccount {
  id String @id @default(cuid())

  /// The case this account was opened for. Unique — one account per case — and the
  /// **resume key**: an attorney who abandons the hosted flow and comes back must land
  /// on the account already created for this case, not a second one.
  ///
  /// Deliberately distinct from `Case.payoutAccountId`, which is the destination the
  /// case is *bound* to. The two point at each other in the ordinary path and differ
  /// in exactly the two situations that need the distinction: between creating an
  /// account and opening donations (created, not yet bound), and after a change of
  /// counsel, where the new firm's account is opened while the old one still holds
  /// what was already raised.
  ///
  /// Nullable only for accounts predating per-case onboarding — one firm-wide account
  /// with cases already bound to it. Every account created from now on has one.
  /// `SetNull`, not `Cascade`: a hard-deleted case must not take an account that
  /// received real money with it.
  case   Case?   @relation("PayoutAccountCase", fields: [caseId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)
  caseId String? @unique

  /// The attorney who onboarded and holds this account. **Not unique** — one attorney
  /// holds one of these per case they represent.
  user   User   @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  userId String

  /// Stripe's `acct_…` id. The only column not derived from Stripe.
  stripeAccountId String @unique

  /// Nothing is outstanding on Stripe's side — derived from there being no
  /// requirement entry awaiting the holder. Deliberately separate from
  /// `transfersEnabled`: someone can complete the hosted flow and still be held
  /// for review, so this answers "should we keep nagging them" while
  /// `transfersEnabled` answers "can a case take money". Conflating the two opens
  /// donations against an account that cannot receive them.
  detailsSubmitted Boolean @default(false)
  /// **The donation gate.** Nothing may be charged toward a case whose bound
  /// account does not have this true.
  ///
  /// Mirrors the Accounts v2 `recipient` capability
  /// `stripe_balance.stripe_transfers` — the one that lets an account *receive* a
  /// destination transfer. Deliberately not "charges enabled": these accounts
  /// never charge anyone. JustUs is the merchant of record and the recipient only
  /// receives, which is why the `recipient` configuration is requested and
  /// `merchant` is not.
  transfersEnabled Boolean @default(false)
  /// Whether Stripe will pay the balance out to their bank
  /// (`stripe_balance.payouts`). False here means money can still arrive and
  /// simply sits in the Stripe balance — worth surfacing to the holder, but it
  /// does not block donations. Read-only: unlike transfers, it cannot be
  /// requested at creation, only observed.
  payoutsEnabled   Boolean @default(false)

  /// When Stripe last told us about this account. A stale timestamp next to a
  /// half-onboarded account is how a stuck recipient becomes visible instead of
  /// silently never receiving money.
  syncedAt DateTime?

  /// Cases *bound* to this account as their destination. A list rather than one
  /// because the legacy firm-wide accounts still carry several; every account created
  /// per-case has exactly the one it was opened for.
  cases Case[] @relation("CasePayoutDestination")

  createdAt DateTime @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime @default(now()) @updatedAt

  /// "Every account this attorney holds" — the settings screen's query, and what the
  /// nudge counts. Indexed rather than unique since per-case onboarding: Postgres does
  /// not index foreign keys on its own.
  @@index([userId])
  @@map("payout_account")
}

/// Who a given case pays out to.
///
/// **Only `attorney` is written now.** Donations go to the operating account of the
/// firm representing the case; the attorney moves them into trust themselves.
///
///   attorney  — the matched attorney's firm receives. The only value new binds write.
///   plaintiff — **historical.** Paying the case owner directly was the model until
///               the pivot to firm operating accounts. Rows already bound this way
///               keep it: donors were shown that recipient before they gave, and
///               rewriting the column would make a delivered disclosure retroactively
///               false. Nothing writes it any more, and `bindCasePayout` cannot.
///
/// This is the value the **donor-facing disclosure reads from**. A donor is told who
/// receives before they confirm, so it must be set and stated, never inferred at
/// render time — and a legacy `plaintiff` case must keep telling the truth about
/// itself rather than inheriting the new wording.
enum PayoutRecipient {
  plaintiff
  attorney
}

/// Lifecycle of a donation.
///
///   pending   — Checkout Session created, donor has not finished paying. The row
///               exists from this moment so a completed payment always has a row
///               to land on, rather than the webhook inventing one.
///   succeeded — payment captured; the only state that counts toward
///               `Case.raisedCents`.
///   failed    — the donor's payment did not go through, or they abandoned it.
///   refunded  — captured then returned, in full or in part.
enum DonationStatus {
  pending
  succeeded
  failed
  refunded
}

/// Outcome of the donor acknowledgement for one donation — see
/// `DonationAcknowledgement`.
///
///   sent    — handed to the email provider and accepted.
///   skipped — deliberately not sent, and why is on the row. A guest checkout
///             that yielded no email is the usual cause; there is nowhere to
///             send, and that is a fact about the donation, not a failure.
///   failed  — the provider rejected it. Terminal: the reservation is already
///             taken, so nothing retries on its own.
enum DonationAcknowledgementStatus {
  sent
  skipped
  failed
}
